<worksheet>
<title>Quadratic Functions</title>
<page>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Evaluate a function.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Use function notation.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    A <term>function</term> is a relationship where for each value of the input, there is exactly one output value. In terms of algebraic equations, a function is an equation 
    where you can isolate <m>y</m>, the output variable.
</p>
<sidebyside>
    <p>
        <alert>Function:</alert> <m>y = x^2-3x</m>
    </p>
    <p>
        <alert>Non-Function:</alert> <m>x^2+y^2 = 25</m>
    </p>
</sidebyside>
<p>
    For graphs, the graph of a function, passes the <term>vertical line test</term>. That is, every vertical intersects the graph at most one time. The graph of an equation,
    that is not a function, is said to fail the <term>vertical line test</term> when a vertical line intersects the graph more than once.
</p>
<sidebyside widths = "45% 10% 45%">
    <figure xml:id="functiongraph">
        <caption>The graph of the function <m>y=x^2-3x</m></caption>
        <image width="75%">
        <shortdescription>The graph of the function y=x^2-3x</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
          <definition> f(x) = x^2-3*x</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" bounding-box="yes"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
        
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="nonfunctiongraph">
        <caption>The graph of the equation <m>x^2+y^2=25</m></caption>
        <image idth="75%">
        <shortdescription>A circle of radius 5 centered at the origin</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-97g2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)">
          <coordinates xlabel="x" ylabel="y" bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
            <grid-axes bounding-box="yes"/>
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="5"/>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
</sidebyside>
<p>
    When you input a value into a function it is called <term>evaluating</term> a function. For example, if you let <m>x=2</m> for the function <m>y=x^2-3x</m>, then
    <m>y = 2^2-3(2) = -2</m>.
</p>
<p>
    It can be awkward to write <q>Let <m>x=</m>...</q> every time you want to evaluate a function. Instead we can use function notation. <term>Function notation</term> replaces 
    <m>y</m> with <m>f(x)</m>, where <m>f</m> stands for function. Now we can simply replace the <m>x</m> in the parentheses with a value to indicate that we want to evaluate
    the function and then replace the <m>x</m>'s on the right side with the same value.
</p>
<example>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Evaluate <m>f(3)</m> for <m>f(x) = 3x^2-5x+4</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <m>f(3) = 3(3)^2-5(3)+4=16</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Evaluate the quadratic function <m>f(x) = -2x^2+9x-6</m> at the following values:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>f(2)</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>f(-1)</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>f(0)</m> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>f(25)</m> (Use your calculator!)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>f(-12.47)</m> (Use your calculator!)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>